+# rubric_judge
+
+Rewards free-form responses with an LLM judge, for domains with no verifiable answer.
+
+For every response the server fills each rubric in `rubric_weights` with the question and the
+response, asks the judge for a one-token verdict from 1 to 5, and takes the expected verdict
+under the judge's first-token distribution. The reward is the weighted mean of those scores,
+scaled to [0, 1]. The scores are continuous, so rollouts of one prompt rarely tie.
+
+The rubrics in `prompts/rubrics.yaml` (helpfulness, instruction following, truthfulness and
+honesty) are adapted from UltraFeedback through
+[ActiveUltraFeedback](https://github.com/lasgroup/ActiveUltraFeedback).
+
+## Rows
+
+Each row needs the bare request in `question`, next to the usual `responses_create_params`:
+
+```json
+{"responses_create_params": {"input": [{"role": "user", "content": "Why is the sky blue?"}]},
+ "question": "Why is the sky blue?",
+ "agent_ref": {"type": "responses_api_agents", "name": "rubric_judge_simple_agent"}}
+```
+
+`question` is what the judge reads, so `input` can wrap it in few-shot examples for a model
+without a chat template.
+
+## Reward
+
+| Case | Reward | Judge calls |
+|---|---|---|
+| Normal reply | weighted mean of the rubric scores | one per rubric |
+| Reply cut off by a token limit | `truncated_reward` (default 0) | none |
+| Empty reply | 0 | none |
+| No verdict after `judge_retries` | 0, and flagged `mask_sample` | up to `judge_retries + 1` per rubric |
+
+A judge failure says nothing about the response, so NeMo-RL leaves a flagged sample out of the
+loss. A reply that gives no digit is a failure too, and so is a prompt over the judge's context.
+Per-rubric scores come back as `rubric_<name>` fields.
+
+## Judge
+
+`judge_model` is an ordinary `vllm_model` server. With NeMo-RL, `env.judge_pool` runs the judge
+on nodes of the job's own Ray cluster and fills in its `base_url` and `model`. By hand:
+
+```bash
+gym env start \
+    --config resources_servers/rubric_judge/configs/rubric_judge.yaml \
+    ++judge_model.responses_api_models.vllm_model.base_url=http://<host>:8000/v1 \
+    ++judge_model.responses_api_models.vllm_model.model=<model>
+```
+
+The judge has to return `top_logprobs`, and the first token has to be the verdict, so the
+default config turns thinking off with `chat_template_kwargs`.
+
+## AlpacaEval validation
+
+Rubric scores are what the policy trains on, so `alpaca_eval_judge` validates with AlpacaEval 2.0's
+prompt and weighted win instead: the judge picks the better of the reply and the row's
+`baseline_answer` with a one-token `m` or `M`, and the win is `P(m) / (P(m) + P(M))`. Each reply
+is judged in both orders and the wins averaged, which takes two judge calls per reply. Rows name
+`alpaca_eval_simple_agent` and carry `baseline_answer`. The prompt is
+`prompts/alpaca_eval.yaml`; the judge model is whatever `judge_model` points at. A reply cut off by
+a token limit is judged as it stands, and is only reported in `judge_truncated`.
+
+## Tests
+
+`tests/test_scoring.py` covers the scoring and needs neither Gym nor pytest. `tests/test_app.py`
+covers the server with a stubbed judge.
